package server

const mcpInfrastructurePolicy = "Infrastructure capacity uses infrastructure-resources-v1 in bridge/browser 0.11.1 with the same two MCP tools and Process Simulator endpoints. GET /simulation/capabilities discovers supportedModelSchemaVersions:[1,2] and infrastructure.requiredModelSchemaVersion:2; schemaVersion:1 remains compatibility metadata. Typed pools require model schemaVersion:2 and infrastructure:{schemaVersion:1}, while legacy models remain version 1. Resource.infrastructure defines CPU cores, memory MiB, bandwidth Mbps or energy kW; resource and descriptor units must match. Work.infrastructure defines atomic dedicated CPU reservations and stage/case RAM leases or delivered-energy demand; Edge.infrastructure defines an integer-byte transfer on a typed link with concurrent equal sharing followed by fixed travelSeconds. Case RAM tops up to a per-root/pool maximum, remains held through waits/transit and is shared across forked tokens; stage RAM is per particle. Typed pools cannot be referenced by legacy resourceRequirements. Use complete versioned model PUT or ordinary semantic entity PATCH at baseVersion; omission preserves fields, nested null removes optional fields and arrays replace, followed by strict resolved-model validation. Invalid assumptions return 422 SIMULATION_INFRASTRUCTURE_INVALID with paths and no partial saves. Inspect state/result.infrastructure for resource-keyed CPU, RAM, link and generation/grid/battery metrics; failureCode may report SIMULATION_INFRASTRUCTURE_STALLED. CPU and energy activities are mutually exclusive, energy Work requires processingSeconds:0, bandwidth scaling is unsupported and energy supply uses generation windows rather than schedules/scaling. Model2 bottlenecks optionally identify methodology:queue-history|blocked-admission|bandwidth-sharing|bandwidth-unavailable|energy-shortfall; resource pressure uses actual admission blockers, active shared transfers, unavailable bandwidth or unmet power without fabricating queue counts. bandwidth-unavailable requires activeTransfers>0 and usedMbps===0; its dimensionless score is activeTransfers, including configured zero or scheduled-off links. No-demand and propagation-only states remain clear. Continuous totals remain raw and compare within max(1e-9 canonical units,1e-9*magnitude); counts, event order, millisecond timestamps and capacities remain exact. Model2 Work and all resource pools use one aggregate logical card; Work capacity is in-flight concurrency, not physical server replicas. Saved scenarios stress the same engine through nested entity overrides; no second clock, backend database, TCP/OS scheduler or AC electrical solver exists. Model version 2 is an execution compatibility boundary: older clients must reject it rather than silently reinterpret it. Infrastructure models are rejected by live collaboration rooms until a compatible document contract is negotiated; share explicit native JSON or encrypted backup instead. Browser lock, local grants and operation recovery remain unchanged."
